### What is batch processing?

Batch processing works on a bounded dataset: a dataset with a known start and end, like all transactions for 2024-10-29. A scheduler kicks off a job, the job reads the entire chunk, performs transformations and aggregations, writes the result, and stops until the next scheduled run. Because the job sees all the data at once, it can do heavy operations like large joins and full re-sorts efficiently.

```java
package com.bank.processing;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class BatchExample {
    record Transaction(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Batch: process the whole day's transactions at once
        List<Transaction> dailyBatch = List.of(
            new Transaction("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("15000")),
            new Transaction("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("5000")),
            new Transaction("CUR3002", new BigDecimal("25000")),
            new Transaction("CUR3002", new BigDecimal("10000")));

        Map<String, BigDecimal> totals = dailyBatch.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                Transaction::accountId,
                Collectors.reducing(BigDecimal.ZERO,
                    Transaction::amount, BigDecimal::add)));

        totals.forEach((acct, sum) ->
            System.out.println(acct + " -> " + sum));
    }
}
```

```text
SAV2001 -> 20000
CUR3002 -> 35000
```

### What is stream processing?

Stream processing works on an unbounded dataset: events keep arriving forever with no natural end. A long-running job consumes events continuously and emits results continuously. Because you never see all the data, computations over time must use windows.

```java
package com.bank.processing;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecord;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecords;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.KafkaConsumer;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Properties;

public class StreamExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put("bootstrap.servers", "kafka:9092");
        props.put("group.id", "fraud-detector");
        props.put("key.deserializer",
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer");
        props.put("value.deserializer",
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer");

        try (KafkaConsumer<String, String> consumer =
                 new KafkaConsumer<>(props)) {
            consumer.subscribe(
                Collections.singletonList("card-swipes"));

            // Continuous: never stops
            while (true) {
                ConsumerRecords<String, String> records =
                    consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(200));
                for (ConsumerRecord<String, String> r : records) {
                    System.out.println("Processing swipe: "
                        + r.value());
                    // Evaluate fraud rules immediately
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### How are batch and stream processing different?

| Aspect | Batch | Stream |
|---|---|---|
| Dataset | Bounded (known start/end) | Unbounded (infinite) |
| Schedule | Periodic (hourly, daily) | Continuous |
| Latency | Minutes to hours | Milliseconds to seconds |
| Throughput | Very high (whole chunk) | High but per-event overhead |
| Complexity | Simpler (complete data) | Harder (late data, ordering) |
| Re-run | Trivial (re-read chunk) | Needs checkpoints, replay |
| Use case | Reports, backfills | Alerts, dashboards, fraud |

```java
package com.bank.processing;

public class ComparisonSummary {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Batch: process 2024-10-29 at midnight");
        System.out.println("  All data present. Simple. Complete.");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Stream: process each swipe as it arrives");
        System.out.println("  Immediate. Complex. Partial view.");
    }
}
```

```text
Batch: process 2024-10-29 at midnight
  All data present. Simple. Complete.

Stream: process each swipe as it arrives
  Immediate. Complex. Partial view.
```

### What are event-time, processing-time, and watermarks?

Streaming must deal with event-time versus processing-time and late data, which batch mostly sidesteps. Event-time is when the event actually occurred; processing-time is when the system sees it. Watermarks are progress markers that tell the engine "all events up to this time have likely arrived", allowing windows to close.

```java
package com.bank.processing;

import java.time.Instant;

public class EventTimeVsProcessingTime {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Instant eventTime = Instant.parse(
            "2024-10-29T09:15:00Z");       // when swipe happened
        Instant processingTime = Instant.parse(
            "2024-10-29T09:15:03Z");       // when system received it

        System.out.println("Event time:      " + eventTime);
        System.out.println("Processing time: " + processingTime);
        System.out.println("Delay:           3 seconds");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Watermark at 09:15:00 means:");
        System.out.println("  All events before 09:15:00"
            + " have likely arrived.");
        System.out.println("  Windows ending at 09:15:00 can close.");
    }
}
```

```text
Event time:      2024-10-29T09:15:00Z
Processing time: 2024-10-29T09:15:03Z
Delay:           3 seconds

Watermark at 09:15:00 means:
  All events before 09:15:00 have likely arrived.
  Windows ending at 09:15:00 can close.
```

### What is micro-batch vs record-at-a-time?

Spark Structured Streaming uses micro-batch: it treats the stream as a series of tiny bounded batches triggered at short intervals. This reuses Spark's optimizer and gives high throughput with a small latency floor. Flink uses record-at-a-time: each event flows through operators individually for the lowest latency.

```python
# Spark Structured Streaming: micro-batch
df = spark.readStream.format("kafka") \
    .option("kafka.bootstrap.servers", "kafka:9092") \
    .option("subscribe", "card-swipes") \
    .load()

result = df.groupBy("account_id").count()
result.writeStream \
    .outputMode("complete") \
    .format("console") \
    .trigger(processingTime="2 seconds") \
    .start()
# Latency floor: ~2 seconds (trigger interval)
```

```text
# Flink: record-at-a-time
# Each card swipe processed individually
# Latency: low milliseconds
# But more complex to operate
```

### What are Lambda and Kappa architectures?

Lambda architecture combines a batch layer for complete, correct results with a speed (streaming) layer for low-latency approximate results. Kappa architecture uses stream-only: treat everything as a stream and simplify by eliminating the separate batch layer.

```java
package com.bank.processing;

public class LambdaVsKappa {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Lambda architecture:");
        System.out.println("  Batch layer: nightly, complete, correct");
        System.out.println("  Speed layer: streaming, fast, approximate");
        System.out.println("  Serving layer: merge both for queries");
        System.out.println("  Downside: maintain two codebases");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Kappa architecture:");
        System.out.println("  Stream-only: everything is a stream");
        System.out.println("  Re-process by replaying the log");
        System.out.println("  Simpler: one codebase");
        System.out.println("  Downside: harder for some batch jobs");
    }
}
```

```text
Lambda architecture:
  Batch layer: nightly, complete, correct
  Speed layer: streaming, fast, approximate
  Serving layer: merge both for queries
  Downside: maintain two codebases

Kappa architecture:
  Stream-only: everything is a stream
  Re-process by replaying the log
  Simpler: one codebase
  Downside: harder for some batch jobs
```

### When should you use batch vs streaming?

Use batch when freshness is not critical and cost and simplicity win, like nightly reports and backfills. Use streaming when the business needs immediate results, like fraud detection and live dashboards. Many banks use both on the same data.

```java
package com.bank.processing;

public class WhenToUse {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Use BATCH for:");
        System.out.println("  - Nightly reconciliation");
        System.out.println("  - Historical backfills");
        System.out.println("  - Regulatory reports");
        System.out.println("  - Cost-sensitive aggregations");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Use STREAMING for:");
        System.out.println("  - Fraud detection (immediate)");
        System.out.println("  - Live dashboards");
        System.out.println("  - Real-time notifications");
        System.out.println("  - Event-driven microservices");
    }
}
```

```text
Use BATCH for:
  - Nightly reconciliation
  - Historical backfills
  - Regulatory reports
  - Cost-sensitive aggregations

Use STREAMING for:
  - Fraud detection (immediate)
  - Live dashboards
  - Real-time notifications
  - Event-driven microservices
```

